  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a structured optimization framework that leverages the observation that optimal sequences for minimizing the AC1 upper bound exhibit regular, hierarchical repetition patterns. This approach combines a precise representation based on hierarchical patterns with stochastic search to navigate the complex optimization landscape. The core idea is that successful sequences often show self-similarity across scales, allowing us to optimize resolutions simultaneously rather than sequentially.\n\nThe central innovation is a multi-scale pattern representation where sequences are built by recurrent convolution. This structure inherently satisfies autocorrelation properties while reducing the combinatorial complexity of direct optimization. The method searches for patterns that, when convolved with themselves at different scales, minimize the maximum autocorrelation coefficient. The algorithm starts with coarse patterns and recursively refines them, projecting updates onto feasible sets that respect the evaluation constraints.\n\nKey advantages include:\n- Controlled complexity through multi-scale decomposition\n- Convergence to low-bias solutions rather than becoming trapped in shallow local optima\n- Guaranteed adherence to bounds by design\n- Progressive improvement through consistency checks between scales\n\nBottlenecks addressed:\n- The direct step-function search failed to further improve on 1.509 despite multiple restarts\n- The autocorrelation structure requires multidimensional optimization that wasn't previously attempted\n- The need for both global exploration and local refinement within a coherent framework\n\nThe plan provides actionable guidance:\n\"This plan suggests a hierarchical, convolution-based representation that evolves patterns across scales. You may modify weights, scale factors, or representation depth where justified. The overall strategy remains: 1) Initialize with coarse patterns 2) Recursively refine via constrained optimization 3) Maintain feasibility through projection. Start monitoring progress after 10-20 seconds of wall time.\"\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe sequence representation is defined by parameters for each hierarchical level:\n- Base pattern (vector of length L): p = [p\u2081, ..., p\u2097]\n- Scaling factor (real number \u22651): s \u2265 1\n- Convolution pattern (real numbers): c = [c\u2081, ..., c\u2098]\n- Probability weights: w\u1d62,\u0175\n- Progressive refinement factors: \u03b4\u2080, \u03b4\u2081, ..., \u03b4\u2099\n\nThe hierarchical sequence q of length n is constructed as:\nq = p\u2080\u03b4\u2080 \u2295 p\u2081\u03b4\u2081 \u2295 ... \u2295 p\u2096\u03b4\u2096\n\nwhere \u2295 denotes convolution, and \u03b4 gains are geometric factors controlling scale-specific relevance.\n\nThe AC1 score is evaluated via the exact formula:\nscore(q) = 2*n*||q*q||_\u221e / (sum(q))\u00b2\n\nwith ||q*q||_\u221e computed over the full convolution.\n\nThe optimization imposes:\n- Non-negativity: all components \u22650\n- Magnitude constraint: max(q) \u2264 1000\n- Representation length: n=400 fixed\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThe algorithm unfolds in three phases:\n\n<ParamsBlock>\nInitializations:\n- L_low = 16\n- L_high = 64\n- Scale_factor_init = [1.0, 1.25, 1.5]\n- w_min = 1e-4\n- w_max = 1.0\n- \u03b4\u2080 = 0.95\n- p_min = 0.1\n- p_max = 10.0\n</ParamsBlock>\n\nPhase1: Sparse Pattern Initiation (20% budget)\n<Steps>\n1. Break current population (S=\u2205) into favorable coarse segments\n2. For i=1 to SCALE_STEPS:\n    a. Select random base_pattern p\u2080 from {uniform, gaussian}\n    b. Set scale s = scale_factor from Scale_factor_init\n    c. Project p\u2080 onto [p_min, p_max] \n    d. Compute quick feasibility estimate (sum checks)\n    e. If feasible, add to population P\n    f. Perform gradient-free descent on pattern hyperparameters\n3. Filter out poorly performing candidates (_score(q) > 3)\n4. Project all patterns to feasible space\n</Steps>\n\nPhase2: Progressive Resolution Enhancement (30% budget)\n<Steps>\n1. For k=1 to RESOLUTION_STEPS:\n    a. Autoencode: use LP to estimate optimal scaling (based on previous scale)\n    b. Formulate consistency constraints across hierarchical levels\n    c. Perform constrained gradient descent (proj-proximal style)\n    d. Regularly validate using exact_score function\n    e. Maintain population of top performers at each scale\n2. Add geometric variants (90\u00b0, 180\u00b0 rotations) to diversity\n</Steps>\n\nPhase3: Opportunistic RefinedSearch (OST convolution)\n<Steps>\n1. When minimal score improvement trends detected:\n    a. Switch to differential evolution with hierarchical constraints\n    b. Use observedSymmetry from successful patterns\n    c. Maintain three search directions simultaneously\n</Steps>\n\nThe core operations implement:\n\n1. FeasibilityCheck(q):\n   return sum(q) >= 0.01 and max(q) <= 1000\n\n2. ProjectToFeasible(q, valuation=None):\n   p = q\n   while not FeasibilityCheck(p):\n      if valuation: adjust weights\n      else: scale components uniformly\n   return p\n\n3. HierarchicalProject(q, levels, hyperparams):\n   enforce consistency across scales\n\n4. AutoEncode(q):\n   constrained matrix formulation\n   return projection to information preserving state\n\nSupport functions:\n\n1. SymmetryExtend(base_pattern, scale, gain=1.2):\n   return pattern-autoextension using gain factor\n\n2. PatternSmoothing(sparse, window=16):\n   recursive truncation and averaging\n\n3. CheckpointVerification(phase, score):\n   compute and store historical performance\n\n4. DynamicRepresentationSwitch(budget, current_score):\n   adapt representation if plateau detected\n\nThe execution ensures:\n- Arrays of exact size n=400 maintained throughout\n- Safe timesteps inserted before expensive operations\n- Best_found preserved independently of progress\n- Coordinated termination before deadline\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n<DefaultParams>\nscale_stEPS = 9\nresolution_STEPS = 15\nOSCILLATION_TRESHOLD = 0.01\nGEOMETRIC_VARIANTS = [0.8, 1.2, 1.4]\nPROJECTION_STRENGTHS = [1.0, 0.85, 0.75]\n</DefaultParams>\n\nSuggested variations:\n- <Variant name=\"sparse_init\">Use minimal pattern length only</Variant>\n- <Variant name=\"full_scale\">Initialize with L=256 base patterns</Variant>\n- <Variant name=\"auto_switch\">Automatically change resolution thresholds</Variant>\n- <Variant name=\"orthogonalization\">Imposing L2 orthogonality across scales</Variant>\n- <Variant name=\"windowed\">Windowed (segmented) hierarchical refinement</Variant>\n\nEntry condition:\nweight in GEOMETRIC_VARIANTS -> try advanced variant\n\nLoad control logic:\n- When.BASELINE_LEVEL * 0.95 < current_score\n- When initialization_complete\n- Otherwise: adjust warm-up parameters\n\nUse fallback when:\n- Any projection fails\n- Local evolution stagnates\n- Dimension mismatch detected\n</ParamsBlock>\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n<Rusage>\nMemory: ~4*n*size(float) + model_storage \u2248 3.2MB\nTime: Breakdown:\n- Primary iterations: O(scale_steps * resolution_steps) \u2248 9*15\n- Each iteration: O(n log n) for convolutions\n- Validation: O(n\u00b2) per checkpoint\nKey complexity: O(n\u00b2) per optimization check, which for n=400 is acceptable.\n\nProgress monitoring:\n<Timebreaks>\nt0=10\u00b7sec \u2192 Early termination check\nt100=200\u00b7sec \u2192 Critical projection update\nt250=600\u00b7sec \u2192 Auto-variation trigger\nt400=1300\u00b7sec \u2192 Deadline enforcement\n</Timebreaks>\n\nFallback handling:\n<error_recovery>\n[Insert standard fallback handler here: retain initial_state, try alternate initialization]\n</error_recovery>\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n<Certificate>\n1. Feasibility check: All candidate sequences satisfy evaluator constraints\n2. Evaluation: Each candidate validated by exact_score before storage\n3. Comparison: Strict fitness comparison (score) \n4. Normalization: Projects all candidates to provide_n=min_remaining_time\n5. Output: Return sequence as list(q), where q = floats of exact length 400\n6. Certificate includes worst-case from { validation runs }\n</Certificate>\n\nEach iteration:\n1. Compute score(optimizer)\n2. Prune if performance below moving threshold\n3. Update representation for next phase\n4. Maintain up-to-date best_found\n\nFinal production steps:\n1. Monitor remaining_time\n2. Exit if deadline reached\n3. Project last best_found to safe format\n4. Return as list(q)",
